+    // GC-eligible once this function returns, and the underlying Swift
+    // object's real deinit — and with it, `AVCaptureSession`'s dealloc —
+    // still only runs whenever Hermes's background GC happens to collect
+    // it, off the capture session's queue, no matter what we call here.
+    // That's the actual crash: AVCaptureSession.dealloc synchronously
+    // iterates its OWN attached outputs and calls
+    // detachFromFigCaptureSession: on each one (AVCaptureOutput.m) — and
+    // that call asserts when it doesn't run on a safe queue, which is
+    // exactly what a GC-thread-triggered dealloc is. But by the time this
+    // function returns, configure([]) above has already made that outputs
+    // set EMPTY, and it did so ON the session's own safe queue — so
+    // whenever dealloc eventually happens, on whatever thread, there is
+    // nothing left in it to detach and nothing left for that assertion to
+    // fire on. The fix isn't forcing WHEN dealloc runs (Nitro/Hermes give
+    // JS no hook for that); it's making sure dealloc has nothing left to do
+    // by the time it runs, regardless of when or on what thread that is.
